# Olist E-commerce Analytics (DuckDB + Power BI)

End-to-end analytics project analysing e-commerce fulfilment, seller performance, and customer satisfaction using SQL (DuckDB) and Power BI.

## Goals
- Build a clean analytics dataset in DuckDB (raw → staging → marts)
- Create a Power BI semantic model and dashboard
- Demonstrate practical analytics engineering habits: reproducible setup, documented assumptions, and version control

## Dataset
Olist Brazilian E-Commerce Dataset (Kaggle). Download as ZIP and extract locally (not committed to Git).

## Project structure
- `sql/` SQL scripts (raw → staging → marts)
- `docs/` Documentation (assumptions, KPIs, data dictionary)
- `powerbi/` Power BI files
- `notes/` Working notes (optional)
- `data_raw/` Raw dataset (gitignored)
- `data/duckdb/` DuckDB database file (gitignored)
- sql/profiling/ Exploratory data profiling queries (row counts, nulls, domains, date ranges)

## Roadmap
- Phase 1: Environment + DuckDB ingestion
- Phase 2: Modelling & Validation (staging + marts)
- Phase 3: Power BI semantic model (star schema, date table, measures)
- Phase 4: Polish (README, screenshots)

## Power BI Semantic Model
The Power BI layer is built on top of DuckDB marts exported as Parquet files.
This phase focuses on semantic correctness rather than visual design.

Key elements:
- Star schema with fact and dimension tables
- Dedicated date dimension for time intelligence
- Explicit relationships and filter directions
- Model-level column hygiene (data types, summarization)
- Centralised DAX measures table

Visualisations are developed only after the semantic model is validated.